  "result": "The bug was an inverted comparison in `Store.save_order` (`shop/storage.py:17`). It's fixed, and all 8 tests pass, including two new ones.\n\n**Root cause:** `save_order` loads the existing rows, removes any old copy of the order being saved, adds the new row and writes the file. The filter was written as `r[\"id\"] == order.id`, so it kept only the matching row and dropped all the others. Each save threw away every other order, so the file never held more than the last one saved.\n\n**Fix:** I changed the comparison to `!=`. It now keeps every other order and replaces only an older copy of the one being saved. Re-saving an order (for example, after its state changes) still updates it rather than creating a duplicate.\n\n**Tests:** I added `tests/test_storage.py` with two tests:\n- Saving two different orders returns both from `load()`.\n- Saving the same order again after moving it to `paid` leaves one row, with state `paid`.",
